WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.936
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.250
CATHERINE D'IGNAZIO:
It's the human behavior

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.320
that is making the
brain of the machine.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.020
That is how you make
the machine intelligent.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.050
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.200
how the future of artificial
intelligence and machine

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.630
learning education might
look a lot more human.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.130
JACOB ANDREAS:
What's the difference

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.770
between saying, you know, a
restaurant review expresses

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.690
positive sentiment,
which is a very

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.318
complicated social phenomenon,
and saying that, you know,

00:00:27.318 --> 00:00:29.610
a picture of the number 9 is
a picture of the number 9,

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.660
which is much less complicated
as a social phenomenon.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.120
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.890
This week, we're
talking with three

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.950
interdisciplinary collaborators
about their mission

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.030
to foster a new kind of approach
to computing technologies.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.830
They've created
an assignment that

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.430
challenges students to take a
critical approach as they build

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.873
the technologies of the future.

00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:54.790
CATHERINE D'IGNAZIO: I
am Catherine D'Ignazio.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.643
I'm an Assistant Professor of
Urban Science and Planning.

00:00:58.643 --> 00:01:00.310
JACOB ANDREAS: My
name is Jacob Andreas.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.680
I'm an Assistant Professor
in the Department

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.685
of Electrical Engineering
and Computer Science

00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:07.060
and also the Computer Science
and Artificial Intelligence

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.303
Laboratory.

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:09.970
HARINI SURESH: My
name is Harini Suresh.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.770
I'm a fifth year PhD
student in Computer Science.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.880
SARAH HANSEN: In spring 2021,
Catherine, Jacob, and Harini

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.760
were brought together as
part of a special initiative

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.300
called SERC from the MIT
Stephen A. Schwarzman

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.310
College of Computing.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.680
SERC stands for the
Social and Ethical

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.698
Responsibilities of Computing.

00:01:31.698 --> 00:01:33.490
CATHERINE D'IGNAZIO:
So the mission broadly

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:38.140
is thinking about how do we
cultivate responsible creators

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.728
of computational
tools and technologies

00:01:41.728 --> 00:01:43.270
for the folks who
are going to go out

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.135
and be building the
tools of the future.

00:01:46.135 --> 00:01:48.250
You know, in a lot
of cases, you don't

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.790
know what the ethical
implications of something

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.160
are until, you
know, a kind of more

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.770
abstract tool or
technology or algorithm

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.870
is kind of plugged
into the human context,

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.860
right-- like that
place where, you know,

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.948
the machine learning
and people meet.

00:02:03.948 --> 00:02:06.490
JACOB ANDREAS: There have been
lots of high profile incidents

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.375
involving things like face
recognition software, people

00:02:09.375 --> 00:02:10.750
trying to deploy
machine learning

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.390
systems in the context of things
like sentencing or recidivism

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.120
prediction, and in many cases,
having sort of seriously

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.100
harmful effects.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.780
SARAH HANSEN: For
Jacob, exploring

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.600
the social
consequences of AI got

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.390
him to start thinking a little
differently in his own survey

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:25.960
course--

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:33.067
6.864 Natural Language
Processing, or NLP for short.

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:34.900
JACOB ANDREAS: The way
we have traditionally

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.960
taught machine learning classes,
it's always, OK, you know,

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.840
here's a data set of
photographs of digits.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.470
Classify this picture as whether
it contains a 0 or a 1 or a 2

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:45.400
or a 3.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.210
And you know, here's a data
set of restaurant reviews.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.710
And they just happen to already
have assigned to them labels

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:52.330
for whether this is
a positive restaurant

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:53.830
review or a negative
restaurant review.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:55.747
You know, go train the
machine learning model.

00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:57.730
And at no point do
you stop and ask, OK,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.902
but where did these
restaurant reviews come from?

00:02:59.902 --> 00:03:02.110
And what's the difference
between saying a restaurant

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.990
review expresses
positive sentiment, which

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.030
is a very complicated
social phenomenon,

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.340
and saying that, you know,
a picture of the number 9

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:12.070
is a picture of the number 9,
which is much less complicated

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.320
as a social phenomenon.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.920
SARAH HANSEN: So he teamed
up with Catherine and Harini

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.200
to show students
that the machine

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:26.480
part of machine learning is
very much influenced by humans.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.170
HARINI SURESH: So the way
that machine learning systems

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:32.920
work in many cases is something
called supervised learning,

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.310
where the data set
contains examples as well

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.510
as labels for those examples.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:42.610
So for example, in content
moderation, you might have--

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:43.840
in your data set--

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.480
comments from a message
board as well as

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.260
annotations that say this
is a toxic comment or this

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.700
is not a toxic comment.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.460
So then what the machine
learning system is trying to do

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.310
is learn from that data What.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.550
Makes up a toxic comment,
what are characteristics

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.900
of toxicity, and it uses the
annotations to figure that out.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.450
And those annotations
typically come from somewhere.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.990
So they might be
automatically generated

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.630
by looking at historically
what sorts of comments

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.490
have been moderated.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:20.290
Or they might be
generated by people.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:23.950
You might crowdsource this or
get specific groups of people

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:26.230
to annotate comments as
to whether they think

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.700
they're toxic or non-toxic.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.680
And those would
become the labels

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.570
in the data set that
the machine learning

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.901
system would learn from.

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:36.220
CATHERINE D'IGNAZIO: We
need to train it basically.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.320
And the way to train
it is you look either,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.072
like Harini said,
at historical data

00:04:40.072 --> 00:04:41.530
or you make your
own data set where

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.530
you and your team or you
and a group of people or you

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.490
hire people to say, this is
toxic, this is not toxic.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.040
Or let's keep this one,
let's not keep that one.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.190
And that-- that annotation--

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.830
that is how you make
the machine intelligent.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.930
And so if we back
up a little bit,

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.810
it's the human
behavior that is making

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.128
the brain of the machine.

00:05:04.128 --> 00:05:06.670
That's why, you know, that step
is really important in there,

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:08.740
that human step.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.020
It's also the step that we
were trying to tune people

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:15.220
into as not just being just
like this objective thing

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.130
that the machine makes up by
itself based on completely

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.873
objective parameters
or whatever.

00:05:20.873 --> 00:05:22.540
JACOB ANDREAS: People
take these classes

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:24.123
and then they go out
in the real world

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.750
and find themselves
building detectors

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:27.190
for even more
complicated things,

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.270
like these toxicity detectors.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.180
And that was what really
felt like the sort

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.038
gap between the way we
were training people

00:05:33.038 --> 00:05:35.455
and the way these tools were
getting deployed in practice.

00:05:38.428 --> 00:05:39.970
SARAH HANSEN:
Together, they designed

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.420
a brand new assignment for
the course, one they hoped

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.090
would get students thinking
about the human element

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:47.740
of machine learning.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.050
First, students
wrote instructions

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.960
for annotating data
sets, and then they

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.875
tried to follow each
other's instructions.

00:05:55.875 --> 00:05:59.120
HARINI SURESH: The overall
goal of the assignment

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.180
was to try and
get students to go

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:07.400
from thinking about data as this
pre-existing, objective, ground

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.790
truth to thinking
about it as the product

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:15.560
of a long and complex process
that involves many steps

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.360
and is driven by human
judgments and values.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:21.890
The goal of this
assignment is not

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:25.310
to say that data is
not useful or that it's

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:30.290
bad but rather to help students
critically think about data

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.980
sets when they receive them
or use them or hear about them

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.270
being used and to help them sort
of ask those questions of who

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.880
was this created by,
how was it created,

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.500
what are its capabilities,
and what are its limitations.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.840
SARAH HANSEN: When Catherine,
Harini, and Jacob started

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.102
reading the students'
responses, they

00:06:50.102 --> 00:06:51.560
realized that the
assignment helped

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.290
students think differently
about the instructions they

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:56.120
were giving annotators.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.570
But it also did something
none of them had expected.

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:01.320
JACOB ANDREAS: The
thing that surprised me

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.778
most was the number
of students who

00:07:02.778 --> 00:07:05.730
said, I've never done
an assignment like this

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.080
in my whole undergraduate
or graduate training, right?

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:09.330
And this is an advanced class.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.430
These are people who
are seniors in college

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.770
or in their first or second
year of graduate school.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.510
And for many people,
it was really

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.927
the first time they'd actually
been asked to sort of think

00:07:17.927 --> 00:07:20.370
about the process by which
these data sets that they've

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.770
been seeing since, you
know, their sophomore year

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.470
were actually being generated.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:28.600
HARINI SURESH: Initially,
we had designed

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:33.260
this to focus on subjectivity
that annotators might have--

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.430
so like subjectivity
in labels in data sets.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.980
But there were a
bunch of other things

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:43.460
that people learned about
the entire data set pipeline.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.670
So for example,
the categorizations

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:48.250
that people came up with
for the same problem

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.380
were drastically
different in some cases.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.700
People were sort of surprised
by how much personal judgment

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.410
they had to use to
decide these things.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.170
Everyone was like,
wow, I was very

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:02.650
surprised by the amount
that I wasn't sure

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.730
and the amount that I had to
sort of rely on my own biases

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:12.350
or judgments to decide what I
actually thought about this.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.600
SARAH HANSEN: And these
students' reflections actually

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.350
pointed to much bigger questions
within the field as a whole.

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:22.230
JACOB ANDREAS: Of the numerous
ways that Harini and Catherine

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.270
mentioned of constructing
these data sets,

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.090
one that has become
I think particularly

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:30.030
important within the machine
learning research community

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.280
these days is
crowdsourcing, where

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:37.049
there's some online portal where
people can log in and sign up

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.510
for a teeny little
labeling task-- like just

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.130
look at this one picture
and tell me whether or not

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:43.440
this is a picture of a cat.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.020
Or look at this one
comment and tell me

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.520
whether it's a toxic
comment or not.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.610
And then you get $0.05 or $0.10
in exchange for doing this

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.500
little micro task.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.960
So there's two sorts of
things to think about when

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:56.792
using these kinds of platforms.

00:08:56.792 --> 00:08:58.500
One is just thinking
about the well-being

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.960
of the annotators
themselves, that it's

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.230
very easy to miss calibrate
the amount of time

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.450
that it takes to do one
of these micro tasks

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.250
and wind up not
actually paying people

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.140
a living wage for doing them.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.660
And there are actually
people all over the world who

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.810
rely on these kinds of
crowdsourcing platforms

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.610
as their primary
source of income.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.830
And another thing--
once you, again,

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:23.070
start to think about tasks like
toxic comment detection or even

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.830
more sensitive things
like recognizing images

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:28.560
of pornography or images
of violence or whatever,

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.655
it is relatively easy-- without
you as the sort of system

00:09:33.655 --> 00:09:36.030
builder yourself having had
to look at any of this data--

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:39.630
to dump just an enormous amount
of like really traumatizing

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:43.920
content on people who you're
paying $0.10 a pop to label.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.280
And there's all kinds of studies
showing that people really

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.790
experience post-traumatic stress
disorder, various other kinds

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.040
of mental health
issues when subjected

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.523
to these kinds of things.

00:09:55.523 --> 00:09:56.940
SARAH HANSEN: In
our conversation,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.420
this notion of context
kept coming up.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.390
The context in which data
are created, pulled from,

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.855
and annotated is
incredibly important

00:10:05.855 --> 00:10:07.980
when thinking about how to
improve machine learning

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.398
systems.

00:10:09.398 --> 00:10:11.190
HARINI SURESH: One
thing that comes to mind

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:14.280
is Desmond Patton's
lab at Columbia.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.910
He works on context
aware annotations

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.810
of social media data.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:24.030
So specifically, the work of his
that I read is around Twitter--

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:27.060
so looking at tweets
and specifically

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.770
tweets in inner city Chicago.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:31.050
And the task that
they're trying to do

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:35.770
is analyze tweets from
gang-involved youth in Chicago.

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.890
And if you look at
some of these tweets,

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.810
they're trying to
annotate them with things

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.540
like whether they're
violent or whether they

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.975
indicate that there's a
violent event going on.

00:10:44.975 --> 00:10:46.350
And if you were
to just read them

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:48.030
without any awareness
of the context

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:49.770
and try to annotate
them, something

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:52.470
that seems like it's super
violent, if you actually

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.680
were in the context and
were part of that community,

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.950
you might know that it's like
a lyric from a local rapper

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.860
or something that requires
a lot of community

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.360
specific expertise.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:07.260
So what they did in this
project was actually

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.250
get experts from the community
to look through this data

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:15.090
and do context aware annotation.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:18.600
And they found that
they were able to do

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.020
a much better analysis
of this data that

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:26.850
was much more accurate and
grounded in the actual context

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:27.820
that it was a part of.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.880
So that's, I think,
one example where,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.190
if you were to just
apply generic tools,

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:35.118
you would really
fail at this task.

00:11:35.118 --> 00:11:37.410
CATHERINE D'IGNAZIO: There
are problems pieces that are

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:41.130
slightly less
culturally fraught,

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:45.840
where there's less room
for stereotypes, bias,

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.450
pre-existing structural
inequalities to enter

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.780
into, like if we're training
a system to recognize numbers

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.525
in an image--

00:11:52.525 --> 00:11:53.400
you know what I mean?

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.612
That is just--
there's less room.

00:11:55.612 --> 00:11:57.570
I mean, obviously there's
still going to always

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.410
be space for interpretation,
but there's less room

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.830
because we don't have
pre-existing conceptions

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:08.850
of number 9's unworthiness or
something like this, right?

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:13.650
But it's more when we enter into
data about using human language

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.770
or that is being used for
decision-making systems that

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:19.330
have real-life consequences
for human beings.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:22.750
So if we're training resume
screeners, for example--

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:25.500
so like systems
where a large company

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.870
would do an automated
system to screen

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.020
resumes and then only
put the top ones up

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.180
to the humans or whatever.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.330
We're baking in a lot of
biases in the process,

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.710
and that has to do with where
the data are coming from.

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:40.877
And it also has to do with
who are labeling the data

00:12:40.877 --> 00:12:44.310
and then who are developing
the technologies.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.742
And it's not because
they're evil, you know.

00:12:46.742 --> 00:12:48.450
Like, it's not because
there's bad people

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.340
at all stages of this pipeline.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:54.690
It's that we haven't
sufficiently kind of trained

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.430
people along the way.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.790
There are tools to deal
with bias, stereotypes,

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:01.320
structural inequalities.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.490
But they just-- they come
from other disciplines.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:05.700
So how do we bring
those things together

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.120
to ultimately develop a more
robust system that works better

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.260
for everybody?

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.770
But just because the data are
not the subject of ground truth

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:16.380
doesn't mean we just
throw up our hands

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.390
or, like, forget about it,
we can never do anything.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:22.800
It just means we have to enter
with more caution and more

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:27.450
transparency and reflexivity
around what are the boundaries

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:32.360
and the applications of the
knowledge that we're producing.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.790
SARAH HANSEN: I asked
Jacob, Harini, and Catherine

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:37.790
what they'd like to hear
from you, our listeners,

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:40.400
about how to help students
take a critical approach

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:41.943
to computing.

00:13:41.943 --> 00:13:43.360
HARINI SURESH: One
question that I

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.550
have that I've
been thinking about

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:49.840
is, what's the right format
to introduce students

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:50.840
to these concerns?

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.440
So in this case, we
did an assignment

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:55.360
within a machine learning class.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:59.140
And I wonder, how
does that compare

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:03.880
to sort of having it be a small
portion of every assignment

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.650
instead of just one assignment?

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:08.380
Or how does that
compare to having

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:11.530
a class that's more
primarily dedicated

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:14.305
to social and ethical concerns?

00:14:14.305 --> 00:14:16.180
CATHERINE D'IGNAZIO:
This is my eternal thing

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.770
with these classes
there's like how

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:22.330
do you weave in the critical
while still giving people--

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:26.500
empowering people with tools
to ultimately change practices

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.020
and also to change the
tools eventually too.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.930
Because the tools don't in
themselves also work perfectly.

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:33.640
They all have
their own politics.

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:36.040
So if people have
ideas about how

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:40.420
do you both build skills
but also have them

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:43.720
interrogating tools,
interrogating politics,

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:46.180
the critical context in
which the tools are used,

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:51.783
I would love feedback
or ideas around that.

00:14:51.783 --> 00:14:53.200
SARAH HANSEN: If
you have insights

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.630
to share about engaging
students in thinking

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:59.380
about the social and ethical
responsibilities in computing,

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:02.470
please get in touch with us
at the link in our show notes.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:05.110
And when you do, you'll be
joining Catherine, Harini,

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:09.610
and Jacob in spotlighting just
how human the digital world is.

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.070
If you're interested in learning
from their open and free

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:15.760
teaching materials or remixing
them in your own teaching,

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.270
you can find them on our
MIT OpenCourseWare website.

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.610
Thank you so much for listening.

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:26.420
Until next time, signing off
from Cambridge, Massachusetts,

